<Item XName="Page" Type="PageMgr" />
<Item XName="ReadMe" UserName="ReadMe" Type="Notes">
<Description>Example text with pretty printing</Description>
<LibName>Eagleware</LibName>
<Selected><T>Bool</T><D>0</D></Selected>
<Text>&lt;P class=MsoTitle style=&quot;BORDER-TOP: medium none; BORDER-RIGHT: medium none; BORDER-BOTTOM: windowtext 0.75pt solid; PADDING-BOTTOM: 1pt; PADDING-TOP: 0in; PADDING-LEFT: 0in; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt; BORDER-LEFT: medium none; PADDING-RIGHT: 0in&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;&lt;FONT size=5 face=Arial&gt;Phased Array Sky Temperature&amp;nbsp;Workspace&lt;/FONT&gt;&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; FONT-FAMILY: Arial; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;Keysight Technologies Example Workspace&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;?xml:namespace prefix = &quot;o&quot; ns = &quot;urn:schemas-microsoft-com:office:office&quot; /&gt;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoNormal style=&quot;TEXT-ALIGN: center; MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot; align=center&gt;&lt;SPAN style=&quot;FONT-SIZE: 10pt; mso-bidi-font-size: 12.0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;o:p&gt;&lt;/o:p&gt;&lt;/SPAN&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;ABSTRACT&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;This workspace demonstrates how to include the effects of noise due to sky temperature in a&amp;nbsp;phased array Rx simulation.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;BACKGROUND&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;Antennas receive signal and noise from the objects they point at. Noise received from objects in sky can have widely varying noise temperatures. In the phased array antenna model the user can choose between a fixed sky temperature value (independent of direction of arrival) or they can configure a sky temperature that varies based on the direction ot arrival.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;BR&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;SETUP&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;Design &lt;EM&gt;Design1&lt;/EM&gt;&amp;nbsp;is a simple 64 element phased array receiver with an LNA and single stage combining. Sweep &lt;EM&gt;Sweep1&lt;/EM&gt; sweeps the Theta angle the array beam is pointing to from 0 degrees to 90 degrees with a step of 10 degrees. Equation &lt;EM&gt;InEqns&lt;/EM&gt; defines a sky temperature distribution. Graph &lt;EM&gt;ArraySkyTemperature &lt;/EM&gt;shows the&amp;nbsp; &lt;EM&gt;ArrayMeas_ArraySkyTemperature&lt;/EM&gt; measurement vs the swept theta angle.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&lt;STRONG&gt;EXERCISES&lt;/STRONG&gt;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;The ArrayAnt model is set up to use a constant sky temperature (Rx Parameters tab, Sky Temperature Mode = &apos;0:Constant&apos;) of 290 degrees Kelvin. Run the sweep and observe the &lt;EM&gt;ArrayMeas_ArraySkyTemperature&lt;/EM&gt; measurement in the graph &lt;EM&gt;ArraySkyTemperature&lt;/EM&gt;. The result should be a flat line at 290 degrees Kelvin.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;Set the ArrayAnt model to a non-constant sky temperature. Double click on it, go to Rx Parameters tab and set Sky Temperature Mode =&amp;nbsp;&apos;1:ThetaPhiGrid&apos;. The Sky Temperature Grid, Theta Grid, and Phi Grid are already set up to the variables defined in &lt;EM&gt;InEqns&lt;/EM&gt;. Run the equation &lt;EM&gt;PlotSkyTemp&lt;/EM&gt; to see the sky temperature variation for different theta/phi angles. As you can see, the sky temperature is hot&amp;nbsp;at theta = 80 degree and phi = 180 degree direction (e.g., assuming it&amp;nbsp;is the direction of the sun)&amp;nbsp;and it is cool at 290K everywhere else. Run the sweep and observe the &lt;EM&gt;ArrayMeas_ArraySkyTemperature&lt;/EM&gt; measurement in the graph &lt;EM&gt;ArraySkyTemperature&lt;/EM&gt;.&amp;nbsp;When you keep phi at 0 degree and scan theta from 0 degree down to 90 degree, the grating lobe comes up from horizon at phi = 180 degree as you increase scan theta angle.&amp;nbsp;The grating lobe&amp;nbsp;receives&amp;nbsp;higher sky temperature from hot source, and as a result,&amp;nbsp;&lt;EM&gt;ArrayMeas_ArraySkyTemperature &lt;/EM&gt;has the&amp;nbsp;highest value&amp;nbsp;when scan theta is close to&amp;nbsp;90 degrees.&lt;/P&gt;&#x000D;&#x000A;&lt;P class=MsoBodyText style=&quot;MARGIN: 0in 0in 0pt&quot;&gt;&amp;nbsp;&lt;/P&gt;</Text>
<Item XName="Page" Type="PageMgr">
<Item XName="Display0" Type="NotesDisp">
<DVport><T>Array_Dbl64</T><D>1;4;;;Wt14;am14</D></DVport>
<PVPort><T>Array_Dbl64</T><D>1;4;;;6YP8cXuCl24;jms2SBO}b24</D></PVPort>
<Show><T>Bool</T><D>1</D></Show>
<ShowCmd><T>Int</T><D>1</D></ShowCmd>
<Window><T>Array_I64</T><D>1;4;;;C6;q4</D></Window>
<ZOrder><T>Int</T><D>1</D></ZOrder></Item></Item></Item>
